+### [Neural Network 3D Object Representation](11278-neural-network-3d-object-representation.md)
+A method where a multilayer feed-forward neural network represents a 3D object by mapping 3D coordinates in object space to a binary output indicating membership. For objects with simple analytical forms (e.g., sphere, cube), specify the network's parameters manually. For complex shapes, generate training examples consisting of points on the object's surface, points on inclosed surfaces, and points on enclosing surfaces. Generate training data using a heuristic that uses the surface normal to determine if a point in the vicinity of the surface belongs to the inside or outside of the object. Train the network on the generated examples using the back propagation technique. Test the representation method by repeated evaluations of the network for points inside the object space.
+
+### [Neural Graph Mapping](11959-neural-graph-mapping.md)
+A dynamic multi-field scene representation composed of small, lightweight neural fields anchored to keyframes in a pose graph. Fields deform with updated poses during loop closure, enabling consistent volumetric mapping without costly reintegration or fixed scene boundaries. Merges accurate sparse visual tracking with dense neural mapping in an RGB-D SLAM framework.
+
+### Neural Anchors
 Self-contained "baked" primitives used during inference where the dependency between the feature vector $f_i$ and the global hash grid position is severed [S2]. During rendering, operations such as rigid transformation or non-rigid deformation are performed by simply updating the explicit parameters, with no requirement for retraining or complex volumetric warping [S2]. Each anchor stores explicit geometry parameters ($\mu_i, \Sigma_i$) and an implicit appearance code $f_i$ [S2]. Unlike implicit NeRF frameworks that clip objects exiting the training volume, Neural Anchors maintain full rendering integrity by decoupling the sampling domain from static bounds [S2]. They can be bound to external physics engines (e.g., via tetrahedral cages or mesh skinning) to drive dynamic simulations including gravity, collisions, and soft-body deformations [S2].
 
-#### Anisotropic Neural Representation via Spherical Harmonics
+### Anisotropic Neural Representation via Spherical Harmonics
 A method that replaces the direct prediction of volume density and latent features with the prediction of Spherical Harmonic (SH) coefficients, allowing the scene geometry to be view-dependent (anisotropic) while keeping the MLP input isotropic (position only) [S3]. The anisotropy regularization loss $L_{aniso}$ is the mean squared norm of the anisotropic density and latent features over $N$ samples: $L_{aniso} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} (\|\sigma^{aniso}(\mathbf{d}_i, \mathbf{k}_i)\|_2^2 + \|\mathbf{e}^{aniso}(\mathbf{d}_i, \mathbf{W}_i)\|_2^2)$ [S3]. The maximal degree of the spherical harmonic basis $L$ is typically 3 [S3]. The output dimension of the first MLP $F_\theta$ is modified to $(L + 1)^2$ times the original output dimension to accommodate the SH coefficients for density and latent features [S3].
 
-#### Streamable Hybrid Representation
+### Streamable Hybrid Representation
 A technique to handle 4D inputs $(p, t)$ in hybrid representations by streaming feature channels along the time dimension using a sliding window, reducing model size and enabling streaming [S4]. For a $T$ frame sequence, array entry $v$ in $V$ has dimension $F + k(T-1)$, where $k$ is newly needed channels per frame [S4]. Temporal interpolation between observed frames $t_s$ and $t_{s+1}$ is enabled by: $v_{p,t} = \frac{t_{s+1}-t}{t_{s+1}-t_s} v_{p,t_{s+1}} + \frac{t-t_s}{t_{s+1}-t_s} v_{p,t_s}$ [S4]. The method applies to any hybrid representation $V$ containing feature vector entries, referred to as the backbone [S4].
 
