+### Component-wise representation strategy
+Assigns different representation methods to different Gaussian attributes based on their signal characteristics: I-NGP for view-dependent color and R-VQ (multi-stage codebooks) for geometry [S7]. Neural fields efficiently represent continuous signals, while VQ works well for repetitive components. Near Gaussians share similar color but not guaranteed similar shape; similar shapes are found across the entire scene. I-NGP fails to successfully train for attributes other than color. Applied in the compact 3D Gaussian representation method to optimize storage and training efficiency by matching representation types to attribute properties [S7].
 
-#### NeuRBF
-A hybrid neural field model that enhances representation accuracy and compactness by combining adaptive radial basis functions (RBFs) with grid-based RBF interpolation [S7]. Introduce multi-frequency sinusoidal composition to extend the frequencies encoded by each basis function [S7]. Decode these features by an MLP for volume rendering with SDF reconstruction capabilities [S7].
+### 3D Gaussian Representation for CT Volumes
+The method represents Computed Tomography (CT) volumes using a 3D Gaussian representation, where the volume is modeled as a collection of 3D Gaussians rather than a voxel grid or implicit field [S8, unofficial]. The CT volume is parameterized by a set of 3D Gaussians, each defined by a mean position, a covariance matrix (or scale and rotation), and an opacity/density value, allowing for differentiable rendering of the volume from arbitrary viewpoints [S8, unofficial].
+
+### Hybrid Scene Representation (HSR)
+A scene representation that decomposes the scene into foreground and background, using efficient voxel-grid representations to query density and color embeddings directly, avoiding computationally intensive MLP inference for density [S9]. Decompose the scene into foreground and background based on points' relative positions to a foreground bounding box. For background locations, query two 4D grids, $G_{bg}^{(den)}$ and $G_{bg}^{(rgb)}$, using the normalized position $\mathbf{x}'$ to obtain density $\sigma$ and feature $\mathbf{f}^{(rgb)}$. Normalize the input position $\mathbf{x}$ to $\mathbf{x}'$ using the infinity norm $r = \|\mathbf{x}\|_\infty$, where $x'_k = x_k / r$ for $k \in \{1, 2, 3\}$. The background grids $G_{bg}$ share the same number of levels $L$ and output dimensions $C$ with the foreground grids $G_{fg}$. Unlike NGP which uses multi-scale hash grids for both density and color (leading to hash collisions and MLP overhead in large scenes), HSR uses explicit voxel grids for density to save computation and memory [S9].
 
